<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nguyễn lords</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1558">1558</a>–<a href="/years/1777">1777</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nguyen lords, Đàng Trong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nguyễn lords were a family of hereditary rulers who governed southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in the name of the Lê emperors from 1558. From their capital at Phú Xuân they resisted the Trịnh lords of the north in wars from 1627 to 1672, and extended Vietnamese settlement into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> delta. Overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a>, their heir founded the Nguyễn dynasty in 1802.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords.jpg" alt="Nguyễn lords" width="320" height="533" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Own work by uploader · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vietnam1650.GIF" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Nguyễn traced their descent from a clan of Thanh Hóa Province that had supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a> in his war of independence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-dang-dung">Mạc Đăng Dung</a> overthrew the emperor Lê Cung Hoàng in 1527, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-kim">Nguyễn Kim</a> and his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-kiem">Trịnh Kiểm</a> led the resistance in the name of the Lê. Nguyễn Kim died on 20 May 1545, the same day he visited the camp of the Mạc commander Dương Chấp Nhất, and his eldest son Nguyễn Uông was then secretly killed by Trịnh Kiểm, who took control of the government.</p><p>Kim's second son Nguyễn Hoàng, fearing the same fate, had his sister ask <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-kiem">Trịnh Kiểm</a> to make him governor of the southern frontier province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuan-hoa">Thuận Hóa</a>, land that had once belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>. He moved there with his family and loyal generals in 1558 and was later made governor of Quảng Nam as well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1600 Nguyễn Hoàng broke off relations with the northern court, arguing that the Trịnh, not the Lê emperor, ruled. He died in 1613 after 55 years in the south. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-tung">Trịnh Tùng</a> had the emperor executed in 1620, his successor Nguyễn Phúc Nguyên refused to send taxes or to recognise the new emperor, and open war with Trịnh Tráng followed in 1627. Peace with Trịnh Tạc began a long era of relative calm between north and south.</p><p>The Nguyễn then turned south. They fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> in 1611, 1629, 1653 and 1692, ruling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> court through the Principality of Thuận Thành, and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> in 1658, 1690, 1691, 1697 and 1713. Vietnamese had been allowed to settle at Prey Nokor, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>, in 1623, and after a campaign begun in 1755 the Nguyễn held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta">Mekong Delta</a> and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Hà Tiên</a>. In 1771 heavy taxes and defeats in Cambodia set off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> uprising; the Trịnh attacked from the north in 1774, and by 1777 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-dinh">Gia Định</a> had fallen and nearly the whole family had been killed except the nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Nguyễn Ánh</a>, who fled to Siam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The realm was called Đàng Trong, the Inner Realm, and by Europeans the Kingdom of Cochinchina. In 1744 Nguyễn Phúc Khoát took the title Nguyễn Vương to match the Trịnh lords. The Nguyễn were more open to Europeans than the Trịnh: the Portuguese set up a trading post at Hội An, and Portuguese engineers helped them cast bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-de-rhodes">Alexandre de Rhodes</a> reached the court at Huế in 1640, but Nguyễn Phúc Lan later sentenced him to death and allowed him to leave.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nguyễn lords” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1627 to 1672 the Trịnh fought seven major campaigns against the <b>Nguyễn lords</b> in the south, using war elephants, large infantry forces and fleets of galleys; the Nguyễn held them off behind fortified walls near Đồng Hới and with cannon cast with Portuguese help, and in 1643…</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nguyen dynasty descended from the <b>Nguyen lords</b>, who had ruled southern Vietnam (Dang Trong) since the sixteenth century, from 1687 with their seat at Phu Xuan, the later Hue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Nguyen lords</b> defended the Dong Hoi walls against Trinh attacks between 1627 and 1672 with the help of Portuguese cannon.</blockquote><a href="/state/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a></li></ul></section>
